Methodology
Following Zeileis, Shah, and Patnaik (2010), the de facto regime of a currency is inferred by regressing its returns on the returns of a basket of major currencies, with all rates measured against a neutral numeraire currency, as in Frankel and Wei (1994). The coefficients are the basket weights: a weight on the US dollar close to one with a high \(R^2\) points to a dollar peg, while small weights and a low \(R^2\) point to a float.
The weights are allowed to change over time. Structural break dates are estimated with the multiple-break framework of Bai and Perron (1998), as implemented in the fxregime R package, and the number of breaks is chosen by the BIC. Each stretch between two breaks is a regime, and for each regime the site reports the estimated weights, the \(R^2\) and the residual variance.
Default settings used on this website:

weekly exchange rate returns, from the bilateral exchange rates published by the BIS (Bank for International Settlements 2026);
numeraire: New Zealand dollar;
basket currencies: USD, JPY, EUR and GBP (a country’s own currency is dropped from its basket);
minimum regime length of 26 weeks and at most 10 breaks, with the number of breaks chosen by BIC.

Every country page lets you download the exact R script and input data used, so each result can be reproduced. Earlier applications of this approach to the role of the renminbi as an anchor currency include Balasubramaniam, Patnaik, and Shah (2011) and Hari Kumar et al. (2020).
